  "source_text": "a b x.\n\tA pairfunction is a function of this algebra into itself, and it can be defined by means of the use of ⟨,⟩, L and R.\n\nREVERSE POLISH NOTATION \t277\nIn Ref. [16],an implementationof unary pairfunctionsin the MAPLE languageis described. In addition, in Ref. [16] the language MAPLE is used in an automatic verification of the equations needed for the description of the semigroup.\nIn this article, we describe an algorithm for symbolic calculations related to the unary pairfunctions semigroup from Ref. [16]. Our algorithm is implemented in the programming languageC++underDOS(so memory limitationsaremoreseriousthaninthecaseofWindows program). This algorithm is based on the extended RPN method investigated in Ref. [5]. The R and their composition. Named composition (denoted by upper case letters I, S, B, M, D, method from Ref. [5] is adjusted according to the expressions containing the operators ⟨,⟩, L, T , C, E, A and Q according to Ref. [16]) is supported too. In addition, the arguments of these functions are lower letter variables or their pairs made by the operator ⟨,⟩. A member of the pair could be another pair, as it is in Ref. [16].\nUsually, such an algorithm is implemented using dynamic data structures, as the linked lists or trees are. If we start with a static array, then we should do some processing using dynamic data structures, and, finally, we get the output in another static array.\nTherefore, our method, supported by properties of the RPN, investigated in Ref. [5], uses no more memory but the starting and resulting static array. Regardless of the efficiency of dynamic memory allocation, we spare some memory and time.\n3.1 \tGlobal View of the Algorithm\nWe assume the processing of the expressions containing the following symbols: ⟨,⟩ input[upp] upp 0 postfix[k] low i postfix[k] ’L’ ’\\0’ i-grasp[i]-1 grasp[i] 1 low i-grasp[i] 0 postfix[j] ’\\0’ 0 i postfix[j] ’>’ ’\\0’",
